Subject: Diagnostic: rollback inputs, replay and outgoing deterministic data
Upstream-base: 9046e67aa2077a7eb4ed6e28c9df3c1d3b368f84

Rollback study. Under RECHARGED_ROLLBACK_TEST the port's rollback (in the
game module) saves the match after every fixed step and goes back a few
steps to run them again. A replayed step must get exactly the inputs the
first run got, so the routers report what they apply before each step
(every machine's packed player inputs and the deterministic data events
they queue for the simulation, in their order), and ApplyRollbackInputs
applies a report again. FixedUpdateTask passes the port a replay step:
those inputs and the simulation, no pads, no capture, nothing sent.

The rollback restores the drawing's objects with the match, so the port
also replays, around the steps, the frames they were played in: what the
frames' other tasks do to the match and its presentation, without drawing,
reading pads or files, sending or making sounds: BeginFrameTask's
presentation, NIS, mega blend and cameras; GameRenderTask's meters,
indicators, darkening, flashes and frame lock (not the blur, whose update
draws); the particles' update;
the front end's presentation, HUD, scenes, lighting, models and stadium; the
event dispatch. The task manager reports every task's time step
(mscharged_rollback_task_time), so that each part gets the one its task had
in each frame, slowed down or not. Replaying the steps alone left all of it
a little further behind at every rollback (a darkened screen flickered).
The port goes back at the start of a frame, before any task of it runs
(nlTaskManager::RunAllTasks): restored mid-frame, the cameras and views a
frame had already set up would draw the current match from an earlier
point of view.

Drawing has nowhere to go during a replay, whose frame was already drawn:
GLView drops any model or packet attached meanwhile. The effects' update
callbacks still run, since they place the emitters (a shot's charge aura
born in a replay would stay where its emitter started, at the kickoff).

Deterministic data the simulation queues to send (player visibility, mega
strike results, NIS loaded) is an input of a later step on every machine:
a replayed step queues none, its data went out when it first ran. While it
waits in the router's queue it lives outside the game's arenas, which a
rollback restores while the router's queue goes on.

The mega strike meter's presses go from the shooter's machine to the others
outside the inputs, in their own messages, which cGame queues for the
shooter's fielder on the other machines. The port records the presses
received before each step and gives them again to its replay
(RollbackRedeliverMegaStrikeMeter); a replayed step sends none, nor the
mega ball pointer of a captain's shot. Nor does it start rumbles: the pads
felt them when the steps first ran, and the rumbles' state stays out of the
rollback with the pads, which PlatPadUpdateTask drives every frame.

The rollback leaves the frame memory alone: it is used afresh every frame,
with its cursors in glxMemory's statics. mscharged_rollback_frame_memory
returns pointers to its blocks. Without RECHARGED_ROLLBACK_TEST nothing
changes.

